When reviewing or improving UI/UX designs in Quasar apps, follow the Apple HIG based design review
process translated to Quasar components:

1. Read SKILL.md in full. It defines Apple's eight design principles, the five audit lenses
   (accessibility, platform conventions, visual design and craft, interaction, writing), the
   report format, and the design improvement process — all mapped to Quasar components.
2. Read references/quasar-components.md to find the Quasar component that implements each HIG
   concept, with props and compliance checklists.
3. Load references/hig/accessibility.md, layout.md, typography.md, color.md, and the platform
   overview (designing-for-ios.md or designing-for-macos.md), plus 3 to 6 files for what is on
   screen. Never load the whole directory.
4. Cite every recommendation as `file.md › Heading` with a short quote.
5. Give every fix in Quasar vocabulary: component name, props, CSS class. Example:
   "Replace custom button with `QBtn unelevated color=\"primary\"`".
6. Tag each finding Critical, High, Medium, or Low, and use the report format from SKILL.md:
   Summary, Critical, Improvements, Craft notes, What works, Platform notes.
7. Cite a guideline or label the point as your own judgment; never invent one.

Trigger this process when the user asks to: review a Quasar design, audit UI/UX in a Vue 3 app,
check accessibility, improve a layout, implement Liquid Glass in Quasar, make a design less generic,
review an app icon, or evaluate any visual or interaction design — especially for apps using
Quasar Framework with Capacitor, Electron, PWA, or SPA.
